# .mobile-workflow.conf — copy to your project root and edit.
#
# This file is the ONLY place a project states policy. Everything else in
# scripts/ is machinery and should not need editing. It is sourced by bash, so
# it is shell, but keep it to assignments.
#
# Every value here can also be overridden for a single run from the environment:
#
#   SIM_NODE_H=820 scripts/mobile/sim-node.sh <udid>

# ── The app ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Used by sim-capture.sh to terminate + relaunch the app in a known state.
BUNDLE_ID="com.example.myapp"

# Optional. Only needed if you wire your own build script through this file.
# SCHEME="MyApp"

# ── The device pool ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# READ THIS EVEN IF YOU SKIP THE REST.
#
# simbroker matches pool membership on the device NAME, exactly, case-sensitive.
# There is no OS filter and no udid list. Two consequences:
#
#   1. A stock name like "iPhone 17 Pro" can exist on two runtimes at once, so a
#      pool listing stock names WILL eventually hand an agent a device on a beta
#      runtime you never meant to support.
#   2. Omitting "models" from ~/.simbroker/config.json puts EVERY simulator on
#      the machine into the pool.
#
# So: create purpose-named devices, and pin the runtime here as a second gate.
# sim-bootstrap.sh creates them from SIM_POOL below and prints the matching
# simbroker config block.

# Substring matched against the runtime identifier, e.g. iOS-26 matches
# com.apple.CoreSimulator.SimRuntime.iOS-26-0. Leave empty for "any runtime" —
# but understand that you are then relying on the pool names alone.
SIM_RUNTIME_MATCH="iOS-26"

# The device used when no udid is passed. Right for one session working alone;
# wrong the moment two sessions run at once, which is exactly what the broker
# exists to prevent. Parallel agents each claim and pass their own udid.
SIM_NAME="MyApp-iPhone17Pro"

# name:deviceType pairs. `xcrun simctl list devicetypes` for the identifiers.
# The names here must match "models".ios in ~/.simbroker/config.json — keep the
# two lists in step, and let sim-bootstrap.sh print the second from the first.
#
# Size the pool to the machine, not to ambition: three booted simulators is
# about all a Mac takes before it thrashes, and that number is also simbroker's
# iOS capacity and therefore the real ceiling on how many agents you can fan out.
SIM_POOL=(
  "MyApp-iPhone17Pro:com.apple.CoreSimulator.SimDeviceType.iPhone-17-Pro"
  "MyApp-iPhone17ProMax:com.apple.CoreSimulator.SimDeviceType.iPhone-17-Pro-Max"
  "MyApp-iPhone16e:com.apple.CoreSimulator.SimDeviceType.iPhone-16e"
)

# ── The canvas node ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The shape a simulator view opens at, in points. Applies to simulator views and
# nothing else.
#
# HEIGHT ALONE DECIDES HOW LARGE THE DEVICE RENDERS. The picture is fitted
# inside the node, so at 686 tall — less about 68 for nodeterm's title bar and
# serve-sim's button row — it gets 618 of height, and 618 at an iPhone's ~0.46
# aspect is 284 across. That is the narrowest the node can be without cropping.
# Width above that costs black bars and nothing else; it is set a little wider
# on purpose so the button row is not cramped. Raising the width will never
# enlarge the simulator.
SIM_NODE_W=352
SIM_NODE_H=686

# How far below the opening terminal the view is seated, in canvas points.
SIM_NODE_GAP=100

# ── Screenshots ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where sim-capture.sh writes PNGs. Keep it inside the project and gitignored.
SHOT_DIR="$PROJECT_ROOT/.build/shots"

# ── Toolchain ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Normally leave this alone: mobile-env.sh asks `xcode-select -p`. Set it only
# if this project must build against a different Xcode than the machine default
# (e.g. a beta installed alongside a release).
#
# DEVELOPER_DIR="/Applications/Xcode-beta.app/Contents/Developer"
